      commonMistakes: {
        id: 'common-mistakes',
        title: 'Common Mistakes When Choosing an AI Note-Taking Plugin',
        content: 'Most disappointment with these tools traces back to picking one for a job it was not built to do, rather than an actual defect in the plugin.',
        subsections: [
          { title: 'Mistake 1: Installing logseq-copilot expecting it to work in Obsidian', content: 'logseq-copilot is a browser extension built specifically for Logseq. It has no connection to Obsidian and installing it will not add anything to an Obsidian vault.' },
          { title: 'Mistake 2: Expecting Smart Connections to answer synthesized questions across many notes', content: 'Smart Connections is built around surfacing related notes, not synthesizing a single answer from many of them. If you want a retrieval-based answer pulled from across your vault, Copilot for Obsidian\'s Vault QA mode is the closer fit.' },
          { title: 'Mistake 3: Using a chat plugin for repeatable template work', content: 'Retyping the same prompt into a chat window every week for the same task (meeting-note expansion, weekly review) is exactly what Text Generator\'s reusable templates are built to replace.' },
          { title: 'Mistake 4: Assuming every plugin requires a cloud API key', content: 'Smart Connections ships with a bundled local embedding model that needs no external API key, and the other four all support connecting to a local model runner like Ollama or LM Studio if you prefer not to use a cloud provider.' },
        ],
      },
